{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"weaponless-textile-cutworm","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\nSplit out of `d26a-8ffa-97cd-4b2a` (`dire-expectable-terrapin`), where these were carried as\nits DEFERRED AC5 and AC6. That bug fixed the key-path DIVERGENCE that made the deployed MCP\nserver sign op-certs it could never verify (Gerrit 2337, merged as\n`83edf9972728ae1e0729a39098ec70e4fab4617a`): verification now resolves the public half from the\nsame ordered chain the signing side resolves the private half.\n\nWhat that fix did NOT add is any GUARD. `sign_manifest` still mints a certificate without\nchecking that the environment can resolve a public counterpart for the key it just signed\nunder, and nothing at deploy/startup fails when it cannot. So the CLASS of defect — a\nsilently uncertifiable attestation — can regress the moment a new deployment shape puts the\nsigning key somewhere the resolution chain does not reach. Signing succeeds; only a later\n`verify_signature` (or a refused claim) reveals the problem, potentially long after the\ncertificates were minted.\n\n## Approach\nTwo independent, additive guards, neither of which changes an existing success path:\n\n1. At MINT: after resolving the signing key, ask the verify-side resolver\n   (`_opcert_own_public_keys`, `src/rebar/_opcert_signing.py`) whether a public counterpart is\n   resolvable for it. If not, emit an explicit WARNING naming the resolved key path and the\n   principal. It must NOT refuse — refusing would break every existing sign flow and is a\n   behaviour change beyond this bug's scope.\n2. At STARTUP/DEPLOY: the MCP server's existing health surface (`src/rebar/_mcp_health.py`)\n   fails when the bound signing key has no usable public counterpart, so the condition is\n   caught before any certificate is minted rather than after.\n\nBoth are in-process checks driven by an operation and by server startup, with no dependency on\nany particular CI provider.\n\n## Scope\n- In scope: the mint-time warning and the startup health check.\n- Out of scope: the key-resolution chain itself (already fixed by 2337); any change to the\n  cross-environment `foreign_key` verdict, which is documented intended behaviour (ADR 0104),\n  not a defect.\n\n## Testing\nUnit tests: a mint under a key whose public half cannot be resolved emits the warning and still\nreturns a signature; a mint under a resolvable key emits no warning; the startup check fails for\nthe unresolvable key and passes for the resolvable one.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] AC1: `sign_manifest` emits an explicit WARNING — naming the resolved key path and the principal — when the environment cannot resolve a public counterpart for the key it just signed under, and still returns the signature (no refusal, no existing sign flow broken).\n- [ ] AC2: `sign_manifest` emits no such warning when the public counterpart IS resolvable.\n- [ ] AC3: the MCP server's startup/deploy health check fails when the signing key has no usable public counterpart, and passes when it has one.\n- [ ] AC4: both guards are in-process and operation-linked, with no dependency on a specific CI provider.\n\n## Definition of Done\n- [ ] DD1: AC1 and AC2 covered by a test that is RED before the change and GREEN after it.\n- [ ] DD2: AC3 covered by a test over the startup health check.\n","id":"879b-9bf0-86fd-4a6b","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":["opcert","mcp","deploy","attestation"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"No guard surfaces a signing key with no usable public counterpart: sign_manifest stays silent and startup does not fail"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787958889453970705,"uuid":"c881eb48-df0e-43aa-a4a2-295746b9bf59"}